\title{Dynamic Programming}
\date{2026-10-27T13:27:11Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{draft}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{We now turn to study "dynamic programming"---which is really neither dynamic, nor programming (at least not in the current sense).  The name was [chosen by Richard Bellman in 1950 as something that sounded good to funding agencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_programming#History_of_the_name); but the name stuck!}

\p{Previously we studied the [divide and conquer](00PJ) technique, where a problem gets broken down recursively into subproblems. We’re still considering the same phenomenon—dynamic programming is about what to do when the recursive subproblems \em{overlap}. A naïve recursive implementation will thus end up being called on the same inputs multiple times, which is wasteful (sometimes dramatically so!).  The basic idea is to \em{save} answers to recursive subproblems (\em{memoization}) so we don’t have to compute them more than once. Dynamic programming has a reputation for being difficult and confusing, but at heart it’s just this simple idea.}

